  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Find the smallest whole number that is larger than the sum\n\n\\[2\\tfrac12 + 3\\tfrac13 + 4\\tfrac14 + 5\\tfrac15.\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(14\\)",
    "B": "\\(15\\)",
    "C": "\\(16\\)",
    "D": "\\(17\\)",
    "E": "\\(18\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Add the whole-number parts first, then use a common denominator of \\(60\\) for the fractional parts. Calling the sum \\(S\\), we get\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS&=(2+3+4+5)+\\left(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15\\right)\\\\\n&=14+\\frac{30+20+15+12}{60}\\\\\n&=15+\\frac{17}{60}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(0<\\frac{17}{60}<1\\), the sum lies strictly between \\(15\\) and \\(16\\). Thus the smallest whole number larger than the sum is \\(16\\), choice C.",
